WEBVTT

00:00.520 --> 00:01.770
We're on part b.

00:01.770 --> 00:05.940
Write the first four non-zero
terms of the Taylor series

00:05.940 --> 00:09.200
for cosine of x
about x equals 0.

00:09.200 --> 00:12.420
Use this series and the
series for sine of x squared,

00:12.420 --> 00:15.440
found in part A, to write
the first four non-zero terms

00:15.440 --> 00:17.990
of the Taylor series
for f-- so, for this f

00:17.990 --> 00:20.340
right over here--
about x equals 0.

00:20.340 --> 00:22.260
So let's just do the first part.

00:22.260 --> 00:25.450
Let's find the first four
non-zero terms of the Taylor

00:25.450 --> 00:29.240
series for cosine of
x about x equals 0.

00:29.240 --> 00:33.820
So let's just say-- well, we'll
put our formula for the Taylor

00:33.820 --> 00:36.780
series up here around
x is equal to 0.

00:36.780 --> 00:39.320
So we're centering it around
x equals 0 right over here.

00:39.320 --> 00:46.520
And so if we say that h of x
is equal to cosine of x, then

00:46.520 --> 00:51.550
h of 0, is going to
be equal to cosine

00:51.550 --> 00:53.860
of 0, which is equal to 1.

00:53.860 --> 00:56.460
And then, if you
have h prime of x,

00:56.460 --> 00:58.842
that's going to be equal
to negative sine of x.

00:58.842 --> 01:00.050
And you could look over here.

01:00.050 --> 01:01.270
This is essentially h of x.

01:01.270 --> 01:02.870
And this is going
to be h prime of x.

01:02.870 --> 01:04.459
So it's essentially
one step ahead

01:04.459 --> 01:06.090
of where we were with g of x.

01:06.090 --> 01:09.570
Well, we'll just redo it
over here just so it's clear.

01:09.570 --> 01:12.900
So the first derivative
at 0 is negative sine

01:12.900 --> 01:14.830
of 0, which is just 0.

01:14.830 --> 01:21.344
And then you have the
second derivative of h of x.

01:21.344 --> 01:23.260
So the derivative of
sine of x is cosine of x.

01:23.260 --> 01:24.760
But we have that
negative out front,

01:24.760 --> 01:26.760
so it's negative cosine of x.

01:26.760 --> 01:31.210
So h prime prime of 0 is going
to be equal to cosine of 0

01:31.210 --> 01:31.796
is 1.

01:31.796 --> 01:33.170
You have that
negative out front.

01:33.170 --> 01:34.650
So it's negative 1.

01:34.650 --> 01:39.740
And then you have the
third derivative of h

01:39.740 --> 01:42.680
is equal to derivative
of negative cosine of x

01:42.680 --> 01:46.080
is positive sine of x.

01:46.080 --> 01:52.630
So the third derivative at
0 is going to be equal to 0.

01:52.630 --> 01:54.410
And then you take the
derivative of this,

01:54.410 --> 01:56.290
you're going to get
cosine of x again.

01:56.290 --> 01:57.650
So then the cycle starts again.

01:57.650 --> 02:05.920
So I could write, this is also
equal to the fourth derivative

02:05.920 --> 02:06.460
at 0.

02:06.460 --> 02:08.960
Right, you take-- let me just
make it clear what I'm saying.

02:08.960 --> 02:11.430
If I take the derivative
of this, I get cosine of x.

02:11.430 --> 02:14.037
The fourth derivative of
h is cosine of x again.

02:14.037 --> 02:15.620
So the fourth
derivative at 0 is going

02:15.620 --> 02:17.786
to be the same thing as the
function evaluated at 0,

02:17.786 --> 02:18.720
which is 1.

02:18.720 --> 02:23.860
And so the fifth derivative
at 0 is going to be 0.

02:23.860 --> 02:27.780
The sixth derivative at 0
is going to be negative 1.

02:27.780 --> 02:31.400
And then the seventh
the derivative at 0

02:31.400 --> 02:33.070
is going to be 0.

02:33.070 --> 02:34.240
So that's enough, now.

02:34.240 --> 02:37.050
I think we definitely
have four non-zero terms.

02:37.050 --> 02:40.450
We have this term-- let me
pick a nice color here--

02:40.450 --> 02:42.870
we have this term, because
we have a 1 coefficient.

02:42.870 --> 02:45.390
This is going to be a 0 because
we have a 0 coefficient.

02:45.390 --> 02:46.870
This is a non-zero term.

02:46.870 --> 02:49.470
Then this is a non-zero term
because we have the 1 again.

02:49.470 --> 02:53.040
And then this is a non-zero term
because we have the negative 1.

02:53.040 --> 02:59.430
So we could say that cosine
of x is approximately

02:59.430 --> 03:01.600
equal to-- because we're
just approximating it

03:01.600 --> 03:04.360
with the first four
non-zero terms.

03:04.360 --> 03:08.130
So f of 0 is 1.

03:08.130 --> 03:10.010
So that's this term
right over here.

03:10.010 --> 03:12.121
Instead of f of x,
we're calling it

03:12.121 --> 03:14.120
h of x right here, so
that we don't get confused

03:14.120 --> 03:16.117
with that original f
defined in the problem,

03:16.117 --> 03:18.200
and that we're going to
work on in the second part

03:18.200 --> 03:20.630
of this section of the problem.

03:20.630 --> 03:23.620
So cosine of x is equal to 1.

03:23.620 --> 03:26.850
h prime of 0 is 0, so
there's no term there.

03:26.850 --> 03:30.240
Then f prime prime of 0, second
derivative, it's negative 1.

03:30.240 --> 03:32.760
So it's negative x
squared over 2 factorial.

03:35.920 --> 03:38.740
And then you get another 0
for the third degree term.

03:38.740 --> 03:40.860
Then you get a 1 for
the fourth degree term.

03:40.860 --> 03:42.030
So plus 1.

03:42.030 --> 03:44.760
But we don't have to write
the 1, it's implicitly there.

03:44.760 --> 03:48.930
Plus 1 times x to the
4th over 4 factorial.

03:48.930 --> 03:52.720
And then the next non-zero term
is this negative 1 over here.

03:52.720 --> 03:55.360
So minus 1-- we can
just write minus--

03:55.360 --> 03:57.080
and that's the
sixth degree term.

03:57.080 --> 04:00.216
So x to the sixth
over 6 factorial.

04:00.216 --> 04:00.840
And we're done.

04:00.840 --> 04:04.660
Those are the first four
non-zero terms of cosine of x.

04:04.660 --> 04:07.787
Now let's do the second
part of the problem.

04:07.787 --> 04:08.870
So we did this first part.

04:08.870 --> 04:11.930
Use this series and the
series for sine of x squared,

04:11.930 --> 04:15.190
which we found right over
here, sine of x squared--

04:15.190 --> 04:17.820
actually, I think
we wrote it, this

04:17.820 --> 04:21.470
is the series for sine of x
squared when we simplified it.

04:21.470 --> 04:25.600
Use those-- that was
found in part A--

04:25.600 --> 04:28.220
to write the first four non-zero
terms of the Taylor series

04:28.220 --> 04:30.510
for f about x equals 0.

04:30.510 --> 04:31.830
So where's our function again?

04:31.830 --> 04:34.140
So our function is sine of
x squared plus cosine of x.

04:34.140 --> 04:35.670
Let me rewrite that.

04:35.670 --> 04:47.230
So f of x is equal to sine of
x squared plus cosine of x.

04:47.230 --> 04:49.806
So let's write the first
four non-zero terms.

04:49.806 --> 04:51.680
And you want to start
with the lowest degree.

04:51.680 --> 04:53.749
So you could say
that f of x is going

04:53.749 --> 04:55.790
to be approximately equal
to-- because we're just

04:55.790 --> 04:58.970
approximating it with this
polynomial right over here.

04:58.970 --> 05:04.930
So, from cosine of x-- so
you have this right over here

05:04.930 --> 05:06.540
is cosine of x.

05:06.540 --> 05:07.950
And sine of x is over there.

05:07.950 --> 05:10.750
So between both of these,
the lowest degree term

05:10.750 --> 05:12.900
right over here is this 1.

05:12.900 --> 05:15.450
So I'll write this 1 over here.

05:15.450 --> 05:20.360
And then after that, the next
lowest-- in either of these,

05:20.360 --> 05:21.700
we have no first degree terms.

05:21.700 --> 05:23.340
We have no x terms.

05:23.340 --> 05:26.340
But then, in both of these,
we have an x squared term.

05:26.340 --> 05:29.375
Actually, let me write it this
way to simplify what I'm doing.

05:29.375 --> 05:30.500
Let me just write them out.

05:30.500 --> 05:34.280
So sine of x squared-- let
me do this in a new color.

05:34.280 --> 05:36.390
Sine of x squared is this thing.

05:36.390 --> 05:38.020
This is what we
figured out in part A.

05:38.020 --> 05:41.454
So that is x squared minus
x to the sixth over 3

05:41.454 --> 05:43.620
factorial-- I'm going to
just write the whole thing,

05:43.620 --> 05:46.000
and then later we'll pick
out the first four terms--

05:46.000 --> 05:52.000
plus x to the 10th over 5
factorial minus x to the 14th

05:52.000 --> 05:53.580
over 7 factorial.

05:53.580 --> 05:56.186
So that's the first
four terms of that.

05:56.186 --> 05:57.310
So this isn't exactly that.

05:57.310 --> 05:58.768
That's approximately
equal to that.

05:58.768 --> 06:00.060
So approximation.

06:00.060 --> 06:03.250
And then cosine of x,
we just figured out,

06:03.250 --> 06:09.600
is plus 1 minus x squared
over 2 factorial plus x

06:09.600 --> 06:15.210
to the 4th over 4 factorial
minus x to the 6th over 6

06:15.210 --> 06:16.400
factorial.

06:16.400 --> 06:19.130
Now we can pick out the
first four non-zero terms.

06:19.130 --> 06:21.172
And since we're assuming
this is a Taylor series,

06:21.172 --> 06:23.171
the first term is going
to be the lowest degree,

06:23.171 --> 06:25.750
then we're going to keep
going at higher degree terms.

06:25.750 --> 06:28.070
So the lowest degree
term right over here.

06:28.070 --> 06:32.230
So once again, f of x is going
to be approximately equal to.

06:32.230 --> 06:35.090
The lowest degree term
over here is this 1.

06:35.090 --> 06:36.782
So let's write the 1 over here.

06:36.782 --> 06:38.740
And then, we don't have
any first degree terms.

06:38.740 --> 06:40.781
We don't how many just x
to the firsts over here.

06:40.781 --> 06:42.570
But we have a couple
of x squareds.

06:42.570 --> 06:44.390
So that will actually
be the next term,

06:44.390 --> 06:46.140
because we can add
those x squareds.

06:46.140 --> 06:49.167
So this right over here,
you have x squared-- so let

06:49.167 --> 06:50.250
me just write it this way.

06:50.250 --> 06:57.160
So plus this x squared minus
x squared over 2 factorial.

06:57.160 --> 07:00.270
And x squared over 2 factorial
is just x squared over 2.

07:00.270 --> 07:03.670
So minus, I could
write 1/2 x squared.

07:03.670 --> 07:05.620
And I'll simplify
this in the next step.

07:05.620 --> 07:07.536
That's just going to be
one term, because this

07:07.536 --> 07:10.310
is going to end up
being 1/2 x squared.

07:10.310 --> 07:10.810
All right.

07:10.810 --> 07:12.720
Do we have any third
degree terms here?

07:12.720 --> 07:14.940
We do not have any
third degree terms.

07:14.940 --> 07:17.130
Do we have any fourth
degree terms here?

07:17.130 --> 07:17.860
We do.

07:17.860 --> 07:20.970
We have this character
right over here.

07:20.970 --> 07:22.470
It's a fourth degree term.

07:22.470 --> 07:26.025
So plus x to the 4th
over 4 factorial.

07:29.040 --> 07:33.270
And then finally, do we
have any 5th degree terms?

07:33.270 --> 07:35.430
We don't have any 5th
degree terms, no x

07:35.430 --> 07:36.450
to the 5ths over here.

07:36.450 --> 07:38.070
Do we have any 6th degree terms?

07:38.070 --> 07:38.800
We do.

07:38.800 --> 07:43.610
We have this one and this one.

07:43.610 --> 07:49.089
So this is going to be minus--
there's a negative sign out

07:49.089 --> 07:50.130
in front of both of them.

07:50.130 --> 07:53.900
So it's going to be minus x
to the 6th over 3 factorial

07:53.900 --> 07:57.400
plus x to the 6th
over 6 factorial.

07:57.400 --> 07:59.770
You could say it's minus x
to the 6th over 3 factorial,

07:59.770 --> 08:02.269
and then you could distribute
the negative sign if you want.

08:02.269 --> 08:03.530
I just factored it out.

08:03.530 --> 08:06.320
And now we can just simplify
this thing right over here.

08:06.320 --> 08:10.860
So this is going to be equal
to-- so f of x is approximately

08:10.860 --> 08:16.330
equal to-- 1 plus-- and then,
if you have an x squared

08:16.330 --> 08:18.560
minus half of an x squared,
you're going to have 1/2

08:18.560 --> 08:19.210
x squared.

08:19.210 --> 08:23.390
Or I could just write x squared
over 2, plus x squared over 2.

08:23.390 --> 08:28.860
And then you have, in that blue
color, plus x to the 4th over 4

08:28.860 --> 08:30.150
factorial.

08:30.150 --> 08:36.130
And then, to simplify this,
3 factorial 6 factorial.

08:36.130 --> 08:39.059
So this 6 factorial
is the same thing,

08:39.059 --> 08:41.450
just to make
everything clear to us.

08:41.450 --> 08:46.940
6 factorial is the same thing
as 3 factorial times 4 times

08:46.940 --> 08:53.410
5 times 6, or 3
factorial times 120.

08:53.410 --> 08:55.390
And so what we can do is
multiply the numerator

08:55.390 --> 09:00.370
and the denominator here by
4 times 5 times 6, or by 120.

09:00.370 --> 09:01.710
So let me just rewrite this.

09:01.710 --> 09:03.580
So I'll do this
over here because I

09:03.580 --> 09:05.540
want this to be my final answer.

09:05.540 --> 09:10.080
So if I have x to the
sixth over 3 factorial,

09:10.080 --> 09:13.930
I can multiply the
denominator by 4 times 5 times

09:13.930 --> 09:16.750
6, and then multiply the
numerator times 4 times 5 times

09:16.750 --> 09:20.580
6, which is the same thing
as multiplying it times 120.

09:20.580 --> 09:22.440
So you have 120 up there.

09:22.440 --> 09:24.729
4 times 5 times 6 is also 120.

09:24.729 --> 09:26.270
And now we have the
same denominator,

09:26.270 --> 09:28.620
because this is the same
thing as 6 factorial.

09:28.620 --> 09:31.320
So you have 120 x to
the 6 over 6 factorial

09:31.320 --> 09:37.290
plus x to the 6th over 6
factorial gives you 121 x

09:37.290 --> 09:40.570
to the sixth over 6 factorial.

09:40.570 --> 09:42.470
And we have this
negative sign out here.

09:42.470 --> 09:50.550
So it becomes negative 121 x
to the sixth over 6 factorial.

09:50.550 --> 09:51.740
And we're done.

09:51.740 --> 09:58.630
We've figured out the first four
non-zero terms of our f of x.
